sections.body:
Functions inside an object are called **methods**. Inside a method, `this` is the object that called the method:

```js
const rocket = {
  name: "Eagle",
  fuel: 50,
  refuel(amount) {
    this.fuel += amount;
    return this.fuel;
  },
};

console.log(rocket.refuel(20));  // 70
```

In the call `rocket.refuel(...)`, `this` = `rocket`. Write methods like `refuel(amount) { ... }`; don't use arrow functions as methods, because they don't have their own `this`.

Real programs often use **arrays made of objects**. With **destructuring** you can take an object's keys into variables in one line:

```js
const crew = [
  { name: "Alex", role: "pilot" },
  { name: "Ada", role: "engineer" },
];

for (const member of crew) {
  const { name, role } = member;   // destructuring
  console.log(name, "-", role);
}

// You can also destructure at the start of the loop:
for (const { name } of crew) {
  console.log(name);
}
```

The other way round is easy too: if the variable's name matches the key, you can write it briefly. With `const title = "Homework";`, writing `{ title }` means `{ title: title }`.

examples.code:
const ship = { name: "Eagle", speed: 40000, fuel: 80 };
console.log(ship.name);
ship.fuel -= 20;
ship.color = "silver";
console.log(ship);
for (const key in ship) {
  console.log(key, "→", ship[key]);
}
console.log(Object.keys(ship));

const crew = [
  { name: "Alex", role: "pilot", hours: 120 },
  { name: "Ada", role: "engineer", hours: 95 },
];

let total = 0;
for (const { name, role, hours } of crew) {
  console.log(`${name} (${role}): ${hours} hours`);
  total += hours;
}
console.log("Total flight:", total, "hours");

